It is completely normal to be confused by this. When you have spent your whole life writing standard for loops, suddenly switching to Streams feels like learning to write with your left hand.
Let me tell you a secret: **Product-based companies absolutely love seeing Java Streams in interviews and code reviews.** It shows you write clean, modern, declarative code. Nailing this will be a massive advantage for your transition out of service-based work and into a product role.
Let's build a rock-solid mental model so you never confuse these again. Think of a Java Stream as a **factory conveyor belt**.
### 🏭 The "Factory" Mental Model
 * **stream():** This simply takes your List and dumps it onto the conveyor belt to start the factory line. You *always* start here.
 * **filter(x -> ...):** This is **The Bouncer**. It looks at an item and asks a Yes/No question. If Yes, the item stays on the belt. If No, it gets thrown in the trash.
   * *Golden Rule:* Use filter when you want to change the **number** of items, but keep the items exactly the same.
 * **map(x -> ...):** This is **The Transformer**. It takes an item, puts it in a machine, and completely changes it into something else (like turning a Task object into just a Long ID).
   * *Golden Rule:* Use map when you want to change the **shape or type** of the items, but keep the exact same number of items.
 * **.toList() (or .collect(...)):** This is the **Box at the end of the belt**. It scoops up whatever survived the factory line and puts it into a brand new List.
### 🏋️‍♂️ The "No Confusion" Practice Set
Let's get those clicky mechanical switches going. This practice set is divided perfectly so your brain can isolate exactly what filter does vs. what map does.
Type these out raw.
#### Group 1: The Bouncer (Only using .filter())
*Goal: We are reducing the size of the list. We are NOT changing the data type.*
 1. You have List<Long> taskIds = List.of(5L, 10L, 15L, 20L);. Create a new list called bigTasks that only contains IDs greater than 10L.
 2. You have List<String> devs = List.of("Adesh", "Bob", "Alice");. Create a new list called aTeam that only contains names starting with the letter "A".
 3. You have List<PlanningTask> backlog. Create a new list called criticalTasks that only contains tasks where .getPriority().equals("CRITICAL").
#### Group 2: The Transformer (Only using .map())
*Goal: The size of the list stays the exact same. We are transforming the data type from A to B.*
 4. You have List<String> devs = List.of("Adesh", "Bob");. You want a list of their name lengths. Create a new List<Integer> by mapping each name to .length().
 5. You have List<Long> taskIds = List.of(1L, 2L);. Create a new List<String> by mapping each ID to a string like this: "Task-" + id.
 6. You have a List<PlanningTask> backlog. You don't want the whole task object. Create a new List<Long> that contains *only* the IDs of those tasks by calling .getTaskId().
#### Group 3: The Real World (Combining Both)
*Goal: Filter out the garbage first, THEN transform what survives.*
 7. You have List<String> devs = List.of("Adesh", "Bob", "Alice");. You only care about developers whose names start with "A". For those specific developers, you want to convert their names to entirely UPPERCASE. Create a new List<String> doing this. *(Hint: .filter(...) then .map(...))*.
 8. You have a List<PlanningTask> backlog. Filter out any task that has a priority of "LOW". For the tasks that survive, extract just their estimatedHours into a new List<Double>.
